;; `-?->` as a definition's own arrow.
;;
;; It would say nothing. `-?->` marks a *parameter* the signature will take at
;; either colour; a definition's own colour is not declared at all — a `defun`
;; is already colour-polymorphic, and a copy of it is made for each colour
;; actually used. Writing it here would be a second way to say what `defun`
;; already says, which is one way too many.
;;
;; The case it gets confused with is a leaf whose two halves call *different*
;; .NET methods. That needs two bodies rather than two copies of one body, and
;; that is `defbjouble` — which the message has to say, because someone writing
;; this has a real problem and needs pointing at the form that solves it.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: -?-> says that a *parameter* may be given a function of either colour
;; EXPECT-ERROR: that is defbjouble

(import (std prelude))

(: twice (-?-> int int))
(defun (twice n) (* n 2))

(defun (main args) 0)
